-- Passage rg12-text-structure-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('83c57c22-fcfa-560b-9e2a-ffe9cd86355d', 'rg12-text-structure-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-text-structure'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Moths on the Radar$q$, $q$For years, meteorologists at the Tallis Plains weather station were puzzled by a strange signal. On calm, clear nights in late summer, their radar showed a broad, faint haze spreading across the screen soon after dark and drifting steadily south. It was not rain, since the sky was cloudless. It was not birds, since the haze moved too slowly and too evenly. Most of the staff simply called it clutter and ignored it.

Biologist Femi Adeyemi did not. In 2018 Adeyemi paired the radar records with a set of light traps placed at different heights on a tall communications tower nearby. On nights when the haze appeared, the traps at the highest levels filled with moths, most of them a single species of small brown moth. On nights without the haze, the high traps stayed nearly empty.

The match suggested that the mysterious signal was a migration. Further analysis showed that the moths were climbing several hundred meters into the air, where steady winds blew south in late summer, and then letting those winds carry them. Some traveled more than three hundred kilometers in a single night, a distance they could never cover by flapping alone.

The discovery matters beyond its novelty. Migrating moths pollinate plants along their route and feed bats and birds, so a sharp change in their numbers can ripple through the whole region. Because weather radar runs continuously, Adeyemi argues, it could serve as a free, nightly census of insect travel, revealing declines years before anyone would notice them on the ground.

What once looked like a flaw in the equipment may turn out to be one of its most useful features.$q$, 'draft')
