-- Passage rg12-theme-summary-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('66abf76b-c2d5-521f-bd75-08325eac49d5', 'rg12-theme-summary-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-theme-summary'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Touch No One Saw$q$, $q$The scoring machine at the Harborview fencing tournament had been unreliable all afternoon. Twice it had lit up for touches that never landed, and once it had stayed dark when a blade clearly struck. The referee, a patient retired teacher named Mr. Okonkwo, had begun asking fencers to pause while a technician wiggled the cables.

Marisol Quispe was leading her semifinal bout fourteen to thirteen, one touch from the final she had trained two years to reach. Her opponent, Freya Lindgren, attacked with a long lunge, and Marisol felt the tip press firmly against her shoulder. The machine stayed dark. Mr. Okonkwo looked at the box, then at the two fencers, and raised his hand to restart the action.

Marisol lifted her mask. "She hit me," she said. "Shoulder. I felt it."

The room went quiet in the particular way a gym does when a few dozen people stop talking at once. Mr. Okonkwo asked whether she was certain, and she said she was. He awarded the touch. Fourteen all. On the next action Freya scored again, cleanly, and the machine lit up as if nothing had ever been wrong with it.

Marisol's coach met her at the edge of the strip and said nothing about the call. He handed her a water bottle and told her that her footwork in the second period had been the best he had seen from her. She nodded, unable to answer.

Freya won the final an hour later. During the medal ceremony she stepped down from the top of the podium, walked to the end of the row where Marisol stood with her bronze medal, and shook her hand for a long moment before returning to her place. Afterward a younger fencer from Marisol's club asked whether she regretted speaking up. Marisol thought about the question longer than the girl expected. "I regret losing," she said finally. "I don't regret how."$q$, 'draft')
