-- Passage rg12-word-parts-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ee47fb45-ac2b-5a47-a651-efdc0d8c5d75', 'rg12-word-parts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Organ in the Loft$q$, $q$Marisol Adeyemi had volunteered to help restore the old pipe organ in the town's concert hall, but on the first morning she wondered whether the project was hopeless. When the technician, Mr. Halvorsen, pressed a chord, the instrument let out a cacophony of wheezes, squeaks, and groans that sent a pigeon flapping out of the rafters.

"Dust in the pipes," he said calmly. "And forty years of nobody listening."

For weeks they worked through the ranks of pipes one at a time. Marisol learned to hold a tuning device near each pipe and read its needle, and she kept a notebook of phonetic labels she had invented for each problem sound: "hiss," "buzz," and "the cough." Mr. Halvorsen laughed at her system but soon began using it himself.

The work was slow, and some evenings Marisol felt more like a plumber than a musician. Yet small victories kept her going. When the first full rank of flute pipes finally sounded in tune, the tone was so euphonious that she simply stood still and listened, unwilling to break the quiet that followed.

By spring the organ could play complex polyphonic pieces, in which several independent melodies wove around one another without colliding. Mr. Halvorsen invited Marisol to choose the music for the reopening concert. She picked a piece she had once found impossibly dense, because now she could hear each line separately.

After the concert, a reporter from the school newspaper asked whether she had felt euphoria when the audience rose to applaud. Marisol thought about it for a moment. "Mostly relief," she admitted. "And a little pride in the cough."$q$, 'draft')
