-- Passage rg12-word-parts-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('230ec808-114e-5f94-b5c8-a72e2ad58f48', 'rg12-word-parts-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Diorama Room$q$, $q$On her first day as a summer volunteer at the Kestrel Valley Natural History Museum, Lucia Mbeki felt like a complete novice. The other volunteers spoke easily about specimen labels and archival boxes, while she was still trying to remember which hallway led to the staff kitchen.

Her assignment was the diorama room, a long gallery of glass cases where preserved animals stood in front of painted landscapes. The room was closing for a renovation, and the curator, Dr. Farouk Ainsley, wanted every case photographed and documented before anything was moved. "These scenes are seventy years old," he told her. "Some of the backgrounds were painted by artists nobody remembers. We want to keep what still works and repair the rest."

At first Lucia found it strange to spend whole afternoons among inanimate creatures. The animals were perfectly still, of course, yet the artists had posed them so skillfully that a fox seemed ready to pounce and a heron appeared to be stepping forward into shallow water.

Over the following weeks, she began to notice how each case told a small story. She also noticed problems: faded paint, peeling labels, and a backdrop showing a forest that no longer grows in the valley. She suggested that the new displays incorporate the old paintings into a larger exhibit about how the valley had changed, placing each historical scene beside a photograph of the same place today.

Dr. Ainsley was quiet for a long moment. Then he called it the most innovative idea he had heard all summer and asked her to present it to the planning committee. Lucia walked home that evening and realized she no longer felt like a beginner at all.$q$, 'draft')
