-- Passage rg12-word-parts-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0d56c0a4-d8a4-525e-bc7d-64be0afa701d', 'rg12-word-parts-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-word-parts'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Winter at the Feeder$q$, $q$Anyone who watches a backyard bird feeder through a cold winter soon realizes that it is not a peaceful place. It is a crowded marketplace where dozens of species compete for limited food, and each species has its own strategy.

Chickadees, small and quick, dart in, seize a single seed, and fly to a nearby branch to eat it in safety. Blue jays are more bellicose. They swoop down with loud calls, chase smaller birds away, and sometimes stuff several seeds into their throats before departing. Squirrels, though not invited, are equally determined, and they will climb, leap, and dangle upside down to reach a feeder hung from a thin wire.

Many feeder visitors are omnivorous. In summer they eat insects, spiders, berries, and seeds, but in winter, when insects are scarce, seeds and suet become essential. A small bird may need to eat a remarkable share of its body weight each day simply to stay warm through a freezing night. That is why feeder birds can seem so voracious in the late afternoon, gulping food as fast as they can before darkness falls.

The birds' activity follows a circadian pattern. Most arrive in a burst shortly after sunrise, slow down at midday, and return in force before sunset. Observers who record feeder visits have found that this daily rhythm holds even on cloudy days, when the light hardly changes.

Not every encounter is a contest, though. In the coldest weeks, mixed flocks of chickadees, nuthatches, and titmice travel together, and their mood at the feeder can seem almost convivial, with birds of different species feeding side by side and calling back and forth as if sharing the news of the day.$q$, 'draft')
